\begin{frame}{Discourse about Cajuns}
  Cajun boundaries were marked by Acadian (or European) ancestry and language
  {\scriptsize
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Jack (Cajun): Wilton, il est cadien.
          Il est Chenevert.
          Chenevert, c'est un mot très très cadien. (54:17)
    \item[] \longtrans{Jack (Cajun): Wilton, he's Cajun.
          He's a Chenevert.
          Chenevert, that's a very very Cajun word.}
    \item \alert<2>{Lillian (Cajun): Il est allemand, mais il est blanc, mais il parle cadien.}
          \alert<2>{Il dit, je crois lui il dit il est cadien, mais il connaît son ancêtres sont allemands. (39:13)}
    \item[] \longtrans{\alert<2>{Lillian (Cajun): He's German, but he's White, but he speaks Cajun.}
          \alert<2>{He says, I believe that he says that he's Cajun, but he knows that his ancestors are German.}}
    \item \alert<3>{Bruce (Cajun): Dubois, Gilroy Dubois.}
          \alert<3>{Oui, je crois il a appris, je suis pas sûr à quel âge il allait, et c'était comme ça avec Jack.}
          \alert<3>{Il dit, lui, il a appris le français avec ses grands-parents. (47:15)}
    \item[] \longtrans{\alert<3>{Bruce (Cajun): Dubois, Gilroy Dubois.}
          \alert<3>{Yes, I believe that he learned, I'm not sure at what age he was going, and it was like that with Jack.}
          \alert<3>{He says that he learned French with his grandparents.}}
  \end{enumerate}
  }
\end{frame}